WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.692 --> 00:00:05.815
It's said that people in Louisiana don't tolerate corruption,

00:00:05.817 --> 00:00:06.376
they demand it.

00:00:06.775 --> 00:00:13.460
New Orleans was the first American city that the Sicilian mafia began to inhabit.

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:14.941
I think anybody,

00:00:15.561 --> 00:00:19.625
anybody who has the connections and the money can have somebody hurt.

00:00:50.225 --> 00:00:50.445
Within

00:00:50.525 --> 00:00:52.368
American Italian mafia families,

00:00:52.588 --> 00:00:54.168
the individual with the most power,

00:00:54.308 --> 00:00:54.850
influence,

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:56.271
and wealth is known as the boss.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:01:00.823
Some bosses rule their dynasties from behind the scenes in the shadows,

00:01:00.903 --> 00:01:02.645
always maintaining a low profile.

00:01:03.686 --> 00:01:04.867
Others are highly visible,

00:01:04.947 --> 00:01:07.730
larger-than-life characters with national reputations.

00:01:08.609 --> 00:01:11.873
Such was a longtime supreme ruler of the Mafia of New Orleans,

00:01:12.334 --> 00:01:13.435
Carlos Marcello.

00:01:15.719 --> 00:01:17.460
After centuries of changing owners,

00:01:17.762 --> 00:01:23.025
the Louisiana purchased by Thomas Jefferson finally placed the city of New Orleans under US control.

00:01:24.346 --> 00:01:24.566
In the

00:01:25.307 --> 00:01:30.689
1800s it became a thriving city and the most attractive American destination for Italian immigrants.

00:01:32.010 --> 00:01:33.371
It's very unique in that it's

00:01:33.872 --> 00:01:35.174
90% Sicilian.

00:01:36.174 --> 00:01:43.519
Out of every 1,900 people are from Sicily and it was the first group of immigrants in this country.

00:01:44.560 --> 00:01:46.281
Contrary to what a lot of people think,

00:01:46.341 --> 00:01:46.462
in

00:01:47.042 --> 00:01:50.844
1850 there were more Italians here than in New York.

00:01:52.006 --> 00:01:53.787
It was a one-two punch for about

00:01:54.168 --> 00:01:56.610
30 years and then New York finally overwhelmed us.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:02:02.334
But for the longest time New Orleans was the main headquarters for the Italian immigration.

00:02:03.535 --> 00:02:09.019
The migration to America by Sicilians was precipitated by the unification of Italy in 1860.

00:02:09.823 --> 00:02:13.164
This unification plunged Sicily into an economic depression,

00:02:13.744 --> 00:02:16.485
and the poverty-stricken Sicilians fled to North Africa,

00:02:17.025 --> 00:02:17.725
northern Italy,

00:02:18.006 --> 00:02:19.045
and the United States.

00:02:20.387 --> 00:02:27.447
It starts back with Garibaldi when he went there in 1860 and captured Sicily and then the southern part and turned it over to

00:02:28.027 --> 00:02:33.269
King Victor Emmanuel to become the initial impact of uniting the nations.

00:02:33.669 --> 00:02:34.829
Italy became a country...

00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:37.271
for the first time in 1870,

00:02:37.591 --> 00:02:38.692
since the Roman days.

00:02:39.633 --> 00:02:40.573
And they were promised,

00:02:40.593 --> 00:02:43.856
the southern part of Italy was promised all kind of things.

00:02:44.616 --> 00:02:46.978
And when those promises weren't fulfilled,

00:02:48.159 --> 00:02:48.420
well,

00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.102
they were looking for opportunities elsewhere,

00:02:51.562 --> 00:02:54.664
and the mass immigration started,

00:02:55.286 --> 00:02:56.346
and they took off.

00:02:57.912 --> 00:03:03.776
The migrating Sicilians were drawn to New Orleans because of its temperate weather and the opportunities afforded them there,

00:03:04.515 --> 00:03:06.818
which were different than what New York had to offer.

00:03:07.819 --> 00:03:08.278
In New York,

00:03:08.319 --> 00:03:10.080
they went there and they started digging tunnels,

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:12.461
or they went to work in factories.

00:03:13.262 --> 00:03:14.643
That was not the case here.

00:03:15.162 --> 00:03:16.843
Even in New Orleans,

00:03:17.504 --> 00:03:23.027
they didn't have to live in an eight-story of a tenement building without hot or even water.

00:03:23.168 --> 00:03:24.007
Forget hot water.

00:03:25.368 --> 00:03:26.389
It was a different life.

00:03:26.390 --> 00:03:28.251
It was closer to the European life.

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.132
So when they arrived,

00:03:30.471 --> 00:03:31.673
they almost fit right in.

00:03:31.953 --> 00:03:33.493
It was almost like being home.

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:36.295
It was a natural transfer.

00:03:37.156 --> 00:03:39.098
Another natural transfer to New Orleans,

00:03:39.198 --> 00:03:40.640
albeit a less desirable one,

00:03:40.980 --> 00:03:44.184
was a small but notorious segment of the Sicilian population.

00:03:45.265 --> 00:03:50.189
The secret criminal organization known as the Mafia had been a part of Sicily for years,

00:03:50.689 --> 00:03:53.934
first established as a reaction to oppressive rulers in Italy.

00:03:55.756 --> 00:03:56.236
John H.

00:03:56.296 --> 00:03:56.637
Davis,

00:03:56.737 --> 00:03:58.158
author of Mafia Kingfish,

00:03:58.237 --> 00:03:59.099
explains why.

00:04:00.704 --> 00:04:02.745
New Orleans was the first

00:04:03.205 --> 00:04:08.987
American city that the Sicilian Mafia began to inhabit.

00:04:10.447 --> 00:04:13.448
And long before New York or Chicago or any other.

00:04:14.209 --> 00:04:21.670
They were attracted to New Orleans and to the state of Louisiana because it resembled somewhat

00:04:22.211 --> 00:04:22.612
Sicily.

00:04:24.288 --> 00:04:27.930
And so they came here in droves and by the turn of the century,

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:30.692
we're talking about by 1900,

00:04:31.392 --> 00:04:32.252
there were about

00:04:32.593 --> 00:04:35.314
100 mafiosi in New Orleans.

00:04:36.255 --> 00:04:40.639
The permissive attitudes in New Orleans made the city a haven for vice and corruption.

00:04:41.259 --> 00:04:44.161
The corruption in the New Orleans area is probably going back

00:04:44.682 --> 00:04:46.462
100 years to...

00:04:48.671 --> 00:04:48.871
Late

00:04:49.752 --> 00:04:50.894
1890s that I would know about,

00:04:51.693 --> 00:04:52.694
in the past,

00:04:52.995 --> 00:04:56.336
law enforcement has closed a blind eye to it.

00:04:56.497 --> 00:04:58.338
I'm not saying the police were corrupt,

00:04:59.019 --> 00:04:59.959
but things...

00:05:00.240 --> 00:05:05.244
are tolerated in the French Quarter that would not be tolerated anywhere else in the country.

00:05:06.245 --> 00:05:08.547
When the Sicilian immigrants arrived in New Orleans,

00:05:08.867 --> 00:05:11.689
many became laborers on the docks and on plantations.

00:05:12.629 --> 00:05:15.893
It was in these very areas that the Mafia began to dominate.

00:05:16.932 --> 00:05:17.413
By the early

00:05:18.435 --> 00:05:21.877
1870s, the Mafia had made inroads in controlling the waterfront and markets,

00:05:22.077 --> 00:05:24.338
and eventually the hauling of freight in the ports.

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:28.502
Their means of gaining this control was through their standard way,

00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:29.723
violence.

00:05:30.724 --> 00:05:33.266
Between 1869 and 1889,

00:05:33.707 --> 00:05:38.130
the New Orleans police attributed over 100 murders to the infamous Sicilian Mafia.

00:05:39.392 --> 00:05:41.673
The tactic of gaining control through violence,

00:05:41.733 --> 00:05:43.235
which had worked so well in Sicily,

00:05:43.775 --> 00:05:46.758
became standard procedure in Louisiana as well.

00:05:48.059 --> 00:05:53.664
The violence in New Orleans Mafia-controlled docks and markets erupted as rival families fought for dominance.

00:05:54.432 --> 00:05:55.992
In the ongoing confrontation,

00:05:56.052 --> 00:05:57.872
the supposedly corrupt chief of police,

00:05:57.952 --> 00:05:58.632
David Hennessy,

00:05:58.853 --> 00:05:59.772
was assassinated,

00:06:00.233 --> 00:06:02.473
presumably by members of the warring families.

00:06:03.615 --> 00:06:04.574
Enraged citizens,

00:06:04.634 --> 00:06:06.415
fueled by anti-Italian sentiment,

00:06:06.795 --> 00:06:11.016
took revenge and lynched several Italian suspects in custody in the local jail.

00:06:12.137 --> 00:06:14.937
This resulted in protests by outraged Italians,

00:06:15.337 --> 00:06:16.337
both in the U.S.

00:06:16.778 --> 00:06:17.218
and Italy.

00:06:18.858 --> 00:06:21.839
While the local press rejoiced that the city's mafia had been wiped out,

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:22.939
they were wrong.

00:06:24.103 --> 00:06:25.045
After a couple of years,

00:06:25.045 --> 00:06:26.285
the gangsters regrouped,

00:06:26.324 --> 00:06:26.946
and by the early

00:06:27.725 --> 00:06:30.867
1920s, one family had firmly established its dominance in New Orleans.

00:06:31.807 --> 00:06:37.951
It would eventually bear the name of an illiterate Italian immigrant who became one of America's most powerful mafia bosses.

00:06:38.731 --> 00:06:41.372
Carlos Marcello's parents emigrated to North Africa,

00:06:41.432 --> 00:06:44.274
where Carlos was born in 1910.

00:06:44.295 --> 00:06:45.194
Carlos's father,

00:06:45.274 --> 00:06:46.175
Joseph Marcello,

00:06:46.255 --> 00:06:47.356
went on to Louisiana.

00:06:47.916 --> 00:06:49.538
And when Carlos was eight months old,

00:06:49.697 --> 00:06:53.180
he and his mother rejoined Joseph in what would be their new homeland.

00:06:54.560 --> 00:06:56.100
As their small family expanded,

00:06:56.401 --> 00:06:59.584
the Marcellos took to farming a plot of land outside New Orleans.

00:07:00.604 --> 00:07:01.947
When Carlos was old enough,

00:07:02.186 --> 00:07:06.670
he was given the job of transporting the produce his family grew to the markets in the city.

00:07:08.652 --> 00:07:12.375
Coming in daily contact with the rough world of the Sicilian-dominated markets,

00:07:12.855 --> 00:07:15.399
young Marcello got his first exposure to the mafia.

00:07:16.579 --> 00:07:20.423
It was then he realized the farming life was not going to be his calling.

00:07:21.764 --> 00:07:38.201
Carlos Marcello was an extraordinarily capable and intelligent young man who left his family's farm in the bayous and started committing crimes in the city of New Orleans.

00:07:39.523 --> 00:07:43.147
Marcello's early criminal career consisted mostly of small-time theft.

00:07:44.487 --> 00:07:49.632
A botched bank job eventually landed Carlos behind bars with a nine to 12 year prison sentence.

00:07:50.473 --> 00:07:51.714
At the time of his conviction,

00:07:51.715 --> 00:07:53.036
he was just 20 years old.

00:07:54.056 --> 00:07:55.217
Through a mafia connection,

00:07:55.358 --> 00:07:59.442
Marcello's father was able to gain a pardon for his son from Louisiana's governor,

00:07:59.681 --> 00:07:59.921
O.K.

00:08:00.242 --> 00:08:00.522
Allen.

00:08:02.365 --> 00:08:09.091
Young Carlos learned firsthand the benefits of having friends in high places and the extent to which mafia influence could reach.

00:08:10.315 --> 00:08:11.896
By 1930,

00:08:12.798 --> 00:08:17.841
there were mafiosi at every level of government in New Orleans.

00:08:19.101 --> 00:08:20.822
After his abbreviated prison term,

00:08:21.223 --> 00:08:24.524
Carlos returned to the city and opened a bar called the Brown Bomber,

00:08:24.805 --> 00:08:26.546
named after the fighter Joe Lewis.

00:08:27.627 --> 00:08:33.490
Soon he branched out and began distributing jukeboxes and pinball machines in restaurants around Algiers and Gretna.

00:08:34.631 --> 00:08:40.357
Bar owners who declined doing business with Marcello or his brothers were soon persuaded to change their minds.

00:08:40.638 --> 00:08:42.240
So have you thought about our offer?

00:08:44.381 --> 00:08:44.682
Yeah,

00:08:45.663 --> 00:08:46.703
but like I told you,

00:08:47.765 --> 00:08:49.226
I already have a jukebox.

00:08:49.547 --> 00:08:50.688
Not one of ours.

00:08:51.009 --> 00:08:52.529
Don't have one of our machines.

00:08:52.530 --> 00:08:53.731
You got a piece of junk.

00:08:55.240 --> 00:08:55.480
Look,

00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:58.705
every bar in the parish is putting in our machines.

00:08:59.144 --> 00:09:02.328
I'm sure you don't want to be the only one left with a piece of junk.

00:09:02.690 --> 00:09:04.532
It could be bad for business.

00:09:11.532 --> 00:09:12.511
I think he was feared.

00:09:13.033 --> 00:09:19.634
I don't know how realistic that fear was or how realistic his ability to carry it out,

00:09:20.335 --> 00:09:22.316
but obviously there were many people who thought he could.

00:09:23.336 --> 00:09:23.635
And

00:09:24.155 --> 00:09:30.577
I think anybody who has the connections and the money can have somebody hurt if they want that done.

00:09:38.007 --> 00:09:41.409
Marcello's reputation reached New York mafia boss Frank Costello.

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:44.050
Under attack by officials up north,

00:09:44.491 --> 00:09:47.933
Costello was planning to move his slot machines to the New Orleans area.

00:09:48.553 --> 00:09:59.620
It was going to start in the mafia basically after Costello brought down all the slot machines and he then successfully placed them in all the bars and restaurants.

00:09:59.620 --> 00:10:05.843
He began getting rich because he was getting a cut from every slot machine that he installed.

00:10:06.663 --> 00:10:08.144
He was split with Costello.

00:10:09.745 --> 00:10:13.306
The fact that gambling was illegal in Louisiana didn't deter anyone.

00:10:14.086 --> 00:10:15.767
Louisiana Senator Huey Long,

00:10:16.187 --> 00:10:16.987
agreeing to a 10%

00:10:17.468 --> 00:10:18.187
cut of the take,

00:10:18.468 --> 00:10:20.249
made sure the police looked the other way.

00:10:20.829 --> 00:10:23.170
Marcello wielded his power with an iron hand.

00:10:23.590 --> 00:10:27.531
He allegedly took people to his property on the bayou and had them disposed of.

00:10:28.211 --> 00:10:29.632
And he had a bathtub.

00:10:31.491 --> 00:10:32.592
in back of the property,

00:10:33.393 --> 00:10:35.333
and he'd fill it with lye,

00:10:36.374 --> 00:10:45.038
and the unfortunate victim would be decomposed in the bathtub full of lye,

00:10:45.677 --> 00:10:48.338
and then the tub would be poured into the bayou.

00:10:49.240 --> 00:10:51.380
Not everyone buys the violent reputation.

00:10:52.081 --> 00:10:53.181
As far as

00:10:53.662 --> 00:10:54.062
I knew,

00:10:55.883 --> 00:10:58.343
Marcello did not have a reputation.

00:10:59.860 --> 00:11:00.610
for violence.

00:11:02.163 --> 00:11:15.848
There were allegations years ago that he might have been involved in a gangland style hit on the Mississippi Gulf Coast.

00:11:16.468 --> 00:11:17.748
That was nothing but rumor.

00:11:18.789 --> 00:11:20.309
It never went beyond rumor.

00:11:21.469 --> 00:11:26.371
In my opinion he never had to resort to that type of strong arm techniques.

00:11:26.811 --> 00:11:35.614
New York boss Frank Costello was so pleased with how Marcello ran the slot machine business in New Orleans that when he opened a Las Vegas-style gambling house in Jefferson Parish,

00:11:36.054 --> 00:11:38.414
Carlos was counted in as a 12.5%

00:11:38.534 --> 00:11:38.895
partner.

00:11:40.355 --> 00:11:42.236
By the time he was 37 years old,

00:11:42.376 --> 00:11:45.576
Marcello had amassed a small fortune from his illegal dealings.

00:11:46.537 --> 00:11:47.777
In 1947,

00:11:47.778 --> 00:11:50.478
he became the boss of the entire New Orleans mafia.

00:11:51.378 --> 00:11:54.039
He also became one of the wealthiest men in Louisiana.

00:11:54.619 --> 00:11:57.100
Dozens of illegal and legitimate enterprises.

00:11:59.542 --> 00:12:02.703
Although only 5 feet 2 inches tall and nicknamed the Little Man,

00:12:03.264 --> 00:12:08.206
Marcello became the biggest leader in the New Orleans Mafia and one of Louisiana's wealthiest men.

00:12:09.107 --> 00:12:12.269
His total worth was estimated at $450 million.

00:12:12.309 --> 00:12:14.570
Drugs,

00:12:15.470 --> 00:12:16.971
that was his big staple.

00:12:17.632 --> 00:12:21.514
Heroin would come in from Sicily into the port of New Orleans and

00:12:21.894 --> 00:12:23.775
Carlos would distribute it.

00:12:24.507 --> 00:12:25.227
In Texas,

00:12:25.808 --> 00:12:26.528
Louisiana,

00:12:27.769 --> 00:12:27.949
and

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:31.011
Mississippi, Alabama,

00:12:31.651 --> 00:12:35.073
the whole southern group of states.

00:12:36.434 --> 00:12:41.197
Marcello's territory extended from the Mississippi River Delta throughout much of the Gulf states.

00:12:41.857 --> 00:12:46.900
He became a master at paying off anyone necessary to ensure his operations ran smoothly.

00:12:48.021 --> 00:12:51.703
He was able to keep his activities relatively free of legal interference.

00:12:51.983 --> 00:12:57.366
until concerned citizens invited a former FBI agent to investigate the New Orleans Police Department.

00:12:58.546 --> 00:13:01.668
Aaron Cohen began to expose the goings on in Louisiana,

00:13:01.988 --> 00:13:07.830
which eventually came to the attention of Robert Kennedy and the Senate Committee investigating corruption.

00:13:10.572 --> 00:13:13.893
Since his rise at the top of the New Orleans Mafia in 1947,

00:13:14.594 --> 00:13:17.175
Carlos Marcello's wealth and power continued to grow.

00:13:18.171 --> 00:13:21.613
Although the federal government repeatedly tried to put Marcello out of business,

00:13:21.873 --> 00:13:23.735
he seemed impossible to convict.

00:13:26.396 --> 00:13:27.017
For years,

00:13:27.137 --> 00:13:30.979
he and his mafia family seemed to be able to operate with virtual impunity.

00:13:32.520 --> 00:13:33.561
That began to change,

00:13:33.601 --> 00:13:33.981
however,

00:13:34.341 --> 00:13:43.627
the vigorous anti-racketeering and corruption efforts of individuals such as State Police Superintendent Francis Grevenberg and former FBI agent Aaron Cohn.

00:13:44.531 --> 00:13:48.833
Brought to New Orleans in 1953 to investigate corruption in the police department,

00:13:49.233 --> 00:13:53.115
Cohen eventually headed up the privately funded Metropolitan Crime Commission.

00:13:54.195 --> 00:13:58.157
Grevenberg was appointed in 1952 as superintendent of state police,

00:13:58.397 --> 00:14:01.158
which he says also had a long history of corruption.

00:14:02.619 --> 00:14:04.280
Huey Long had them originally,

00:14:04.460 --> 00:14:06.501
organized it in 1935,

00:14:07.401 --> 00:14:09.302
and he used it like his little private army,

00:14:10.262 --> 00:14:11.843
but they were all politicians.

00:14:12.787 --> 00:14:15.148
People at the politicians asked him to hire,

00:14:15.708 --> 00:14:16.748
and that's the way they did it.

00:14:17.648 --> 00:14:19.029
So I cut that out right away,

00:14:19.749 --> 00:14:21.009
and I fired a few.

00:14:21.049 --> 00:14:22.310
I wish I had fired more,

00:14:23.170 --> 00:14:26.411
because these guys were so used to taking kickbacks,

00:14:27.171 --> 00:14:28.571
and I had a terrible time with it.

00:14:29.172 --> 00:14:30.532
Now age 85,

00:14:30.892 --> 00:14:34.853
Revenberg recalls numerous incidents of police corruption when he was in office.

00:14:35.093 --> 00:14:37.934
I raided a place called the Four Leaf Clover Club.

00:14:38.174 --> 00:14:39.694
I raided it seven times,

00:14:41.075 --> 00:14:41.335
and...

00:14:42.007 --> 00:14:43.387
The fourth time I raided it,

00:14:44.428 --> 00:14:44.768
of course,

00:14:44.769 --> 00:14:46.688
I saw these young girls in there.

00:14:46.768 --> 00:14:50.409
We booked these prostitutes with the sheriff's office,

00:14:50.629 --> 00:14:52.250
but they would never try them.

00:14:53.090 --> 00:14:55.711
The sheriff was half owner of the house of prostitution.

00:14:57.111 --> 00:14:58.331
We finally put the owner,

00:14:58.451 --> 00:15:00.072
the man who was half owner of it,

00:15:00.092 --> 00:15:10.455
with him in jail for 10 years on a mandatory narcotics law that I had passed that liberal judges canceled,

00:15:10.575 --> 00:15:11.055
annulled.

00:15:11.904 --> 00:15:12.685
Two years later.

00:15:14.491 --> 00:15:19.252
The extent of Carlos Marchetto's criminal activities went well beyond the mere control of police.

00:15:20.313 --> 00:15:24.354
Former federal prosecutor Eads Hogue saw how far that influence went.

00:15:25.214 --> 00:15:31.856
He controlled the traditional rackets along the southern Gulf Coast,

00:15:32.196 --> 00:15:33.676
primarily in Louisiana,

00:15:34.356 --> 00:15:35.897
to some degree in Mississippi.

00:15:37.497 --> 00:15:41.258
There were allegations that he had influence even in Texas,

00:15:42.338 --> 00:15:43.939
and by traditional rackets.

00:15:46.268 --> 00:15:49.409
rackets like organized prostitution,

00:15:50.349 --> 00:15:51.250
illegal gambling,

00:15:52.150 --> 00:15:52.930
loan sharking,

00:15:52.970 --> 00:15:54.131
and things of that nature.

00:15:54.751 --> 00:15:57.552
He was involved in so many businesses,

00:15:58.412 --> 00:15:59.353
in gambling,

00:15:59.773 --> 00:16:03.014
in running restaurants,

00:16:04.095 --> 00:16:04.675
in oil.

00:16:05.775 --> 00:16:07.596
He ran a vast conglomerate,

00:16:08.276 --> 00:16:10.057
maybe of 50 different businesses,

00:16:11.418 --> 00:16:12.498
all under his umbrella.

00:16:13.338 --> 00:16:14.579
In his investigations...

00:16:14.987 --> 00:16:18.288
Aaron Cohn learned that all trails led to the Marcello organization.

00:16:19.308 --> 00:16:25.650
Marcello seemed to have both the New Orleans and Jefferson Parish police departments and municipal administrations in his pocket.

00:16:26.830 --> 00:16:30.711
Cohn also discovered that Marcello could be connected to at least three murders,

00:16:31.392 --> 00:16:33.512
none of which Marcello was ever charged with.

00:16:33.532 --> 00:16:37.913
In 1959,

00:16:37.914 --> 00:16:42.275
Cohn was able to bring his findings to the attention of the McClellan Committee and his chief counsel,

00:16:42.375 --> 00:16:43.095
Robert Kennedy.

00:16:44.583 --> 00:16:48.765
The committee was already waging war against the Mafia in New York and Chicago.

00:16:50.166 --> 00:16:53.328
The top mobsters of Louisiana maintain contact with,

00:16:53.928 --> 00:16:55.689
and join in common objectives with,

00:16:56.170 --> 00:16:57.410
their counterparts elsewhere.

00:16:58.331 --> 00:16:59.412
Freely and expensively,

00:16:59.472 --> 00:17:01.993
they move around the country and are visited in turn.

00:17:02.914 --> 00:17:03.454
For years,

00:17:03.614 --> 00:17:05.395
unlike their Mafia predecessors,

00:17:05.795 --> 00:17:11.679
the Marcella Mob has found little need to fight against officials charged with exercising police powers.

00:17:12.283 --> 00:17:17.587
The Carlos Marcellos of today do not battle the long arm of the law,

00:17:17.588 --> 00:17:19.268
for they find that they last longer,

00:17:19.808 --> 00:17:20.409
grow richer,

00:17:20.989 --> 00:17:26.273
and look more respectable by linking arms with willing officers of the law.

00:17:26.813 --> 00:17:29.875
Through Robert Kennedy's efforts in the Justice Department,

00:17:31.536 --> 00:17:35.459
our organized crime and racketeering section really was established.

00:17:36.119 --> 00:17:37.840
That was a Robert Kennedy brainchild,

00:17:38.781 --> 00:17:41.563
to concentrate a group of...

00:17:41.823 --> 00:17:49.127
prosecutors who were specially trained to engage in traditional organized crime investigations.

00:17:50.448 --> 00:17:55.511
Marcello and other mobsters who appeared before the committee refused to acknowledge the existence of the mafia.

00:17:56.331 --> 00:17:57.692
Even FBI Director J.

00:17:57.892 --> 00:17:59.433
Edgar Hoover seemed to concur.

00:18:00.354 --> 00:18:03.175
Hoover had long maintained that the mafia was a myth.

00:18:03.176 --> 00:18:05.396
In 1957,

00:18:05.436 --> 00:18:05.837
however,

00:18:06.397 --> 00:18:09.799
60 mafia leaders were rounded up at a house in upstate New York.

00:18:10.671 --> 00:18:14.972
This seemed to confirm once and for all the existence of a national crime syndicate.

00:18:15.972 --> 00:18:17.073
Absent from this meeting,

00:18:17.133 --> 00:18:17.373
though,

00:18:17.493 --> 00:18:18.773
was Carlos Marcello.

00:18:19.914 --> 00:18:27.396
He had such special privileges within the mafia that he didn't have to attend any of those big conclaves.

00:18:28.376 --> 00:18:28.656
You know,

00:18:29.936 --> 00:18:36.778
there was one at Appalachian where the heads of all the families gathered.

00:18:37.458 --> 00:18:38.899
That was broken up by the police.

00:18:39.535 --> 00:18:43.596
But Marcello wouldn't have to attend those.

00:18:43.597 --> 00:18:45.577
Whatever the reason for Marcello's absence,

00:18:45.737 --> 00:18:48.658
he was still subpoenaed to appear before the McClellan Committee.

00:18:49.398 --> 00:18:54.599
Marcello defiantly pleaded the Fifth Amendment to 66 questions that Robert Kennedy directed toward him.

00:18:55.539 --> 00:19:01.061
His arrogance and contempt for the proceedings provided even more incentive for Robert Kennedy to attack the mafia.

00:19:02.141 --> 00:19:05.622
Marcello even refused to answer the question of where he was born.

00:19:06.482 --> 00:19:08.263
This very withholding of information

00:19:08.363 --> 00:19:11.445
became the weapon that Robert Kennedy would use to go after Marcello.

00:19:12.666 --> 00:19:19.191
Kennedy learned that Marcello had obtained an illegal Guatemalan birth certificate and an entry described him as a citizen of Guatemala.

00:19:20.652 --> 00:19:23.954
Kennedy turned to the one agency Marcello did not have his hands in,

00:19:24.895 --> 00:19:27.136
the Immigration and Naturalization Service.

00:19:28.797 --> 00:19:33.160
He ordered the INS to expel Marcello from the United States without delay.

00:19:35.022 --> 00:19:35.822
On April 14,

00:19:35.823 --> 00:19:37.083
1961.

00:19:37.211 --> 00:19:39.613
Marcello made a routine check-in with immigration.

00:19:39.693 --> 00:19:40.974
He was told to sit down.

00:19:41.855 --> 00:19:44.357
The immigration agent then read a letter to Marcello.

00:19:45.438 --> 00:19:46.399
To Carlos Marcello,

00:19:47.120 --> 00:19:51.524
it has come to the attention of this office that your place of birth is listed as the country of Guatemala,

00:19:52.225 --> 00:19:54.487
and as such you are officially a citizen of that country.

00:19:55.488 --> 00:19:58.210
You are herewith ordered to return to your country of origin immediately.

00:19:59.271 --> 00:20:00.752
What is this all about here?

00:20:00.753 --> 00:20:02.213
You are overdue on your visa.

00:20:02.794 --> 00:20:04.615
Why are you doing this to me?

00:20:04.616 --> 00:20:05.356
What's happening over here?

00:20:05.357 --> 00:20:05.636
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:20:05.637 --> 00:20:09.559
Machado was taken to a waiting car and whisked to the airport and flown to Guatemala City.

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:13.422
The legality of the deportation has always been in question.

00:20:14.824 --> 00:20:17.726
I reviewed all the INS files on that case.

00:20:18.787 --> 00:20:19.747
And let me say this.

00:20:21.309 --> 00:20:26.893
I'm not certain at all that fundamental notions of due process were followed in connection with that arrest.

00:20:27.994 --> 00:20:28.975
He got no hearing.

00:20:29.799 --> 00:20:32.462
He got no opportunity to consult counsel.

00:20:33.643 --> 00:20:34.704
As I understand it,

00:20:34.744 --> 00:20:39.769
his counsel were not able to consult anybody connected with the Justice Department.

00:20:39.949 --> 00:20:41.351
This guy was just swept up,

00:20:42.031 --> 00:20:42.872
taken in a car,

00:20:43.213 --> 00:20:44.174
put on an airplane,

00:20:44.175 --> 00:20:45.094
and flown out of the country.

00:20:55.483 --> 00:20:57.926
Marcello spent two agonizing months in exile.

00:20:58.667 --> 00:21:01.410
After making his way through the rugged Central American jungle,

00:21:01.850 --> 00:21:04.133
Marcello somehow got back to Louisiana.

00:21:05.615 --> 00:21:08.378
How exactly Marcello was able to re-enter the U.S.

00:21:08.578 --> 00:21:09.379
is uncertain.

00:21:10.521 --> 00:21:15.186
Investigator Ed Becker believes Marcello used his connections to sneak back into the country.

00:21:16.083 --> 00:21:22.025
New Orleans fishing boats were down there for the crab and all that,

00:21:22.105 --> 00:21:22.365
you know,

00:21:22.425 --> 00:21:24.065
which they did periodically,

00:21:24.145 --> 00:21:25.686
like once a week at least.

00:21:26.566 --> 00:21:27.386
What did he have to do?

00:21:27.446 --> 00:21:28.267
Make one phone call.

00:21:28.607 --> 00:21:29.067
He said,

00:21:29.547 --> 00:21:31.828
I want to boat here tomorrow at 8 p.m.

00:21:31.908 --> 00:21:35.108
and give it to Joe and whoever,

00:21:35.209 --> 00:21:35.489
you know,

00:21:35.549 --> 00:21:36.369
somebody he trusted.

00:21:36.889 --> 00:21:37.309
That's it.

00:21:39.370 --> 00:21:40.130
And he couldn't take,

00:21:40.270 --> 00:21:40.510
like,

00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:42.731
an airplane back or a drive back.

00:21:43.851 --> 00:21:48.816
getting stopped at the border or driving in flight,

00:21:49.437 --> 00:21:51.058
he naturally wouldn't do that.

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:53.061
As you remember,

00:21:53.121 --> 00:21:54.001
there's always guards,

00:21:54.222 --> 00:21:56.404
always FBI at the airport.

00:21:57.465 --> 00:21:57.585
L.A.

00:21:57.665 --> 00:22:02.710
and New Orleans always photographed the mob when they came off the planes.

00:22:03.311 --> 00:22:06.334
The government-backed deportation efforts seem to have backfired.

00:22:07.115 --> 00:22:13.379
The fact that he could sneak back in made him even more of a hero to the mob than he was before he'd gone,

00:22:14.299 --> 00:22:14.759
before he...

00:22:16.180 --> 00:22:16.300
So,

00:22:18.581 --> 00:22:18.702
well,

00:22:18.742 --> 00:22:21.883
everybody knew he was really the godfather of the United States.

00:22:22.664 --> 00:22:23.324
For Marcello,

00:22:23.364 --> 00:22:26.466
the deportation episode was something he wasn't going to forget.

00:22:27.246 --> 00:22:30.248
His fury toward Kennedy reportedly became an obsession.

00:22:31.489 --> 00:22:35.351
Kennedy also was enraged on hearing that Marcello was able to reenter the country.

00:22:36.279 --> 00:22:39.582
He acted quickly and had Marcello indicted for illegal entry.

00:22:40.423 --> 00:22:45.547
The INS ruled Marcello was an undesirable alien and once again ordered him deported,

00:22:46.008 --> 00:22:48.230
a ruling that Marcello's attorneys appealed.

00:22:48.950 --> 00:22:50.191
They never stopped trying.

00:22:51.072 --> 00:22:51.593
Washington,

00:22:51.594 --> 00:22:51.713
D.C.

00:22:51.853 --> 00:22:53.274
never stopped trying to deport Marcelo.

00:22:54.415 --> 00:22:56.497
We did not get directly involved in that,

00:22:57.177 --> 00:22:58.639
other than whatever they asked us to do.

00:22:59.820 --> 00:23:00.020
But

00:23:00.701 --> 00:23:03.143
Washington really never gave up on deporting him.

00:23:03.523 --> 00:23:09.886
Marcello never forgot the harrowing and humiliating experience of his forced deportation at the hands of Robert Kennedy.

00:23:11.647 --> 00:23:12.048
In the early

00:23:13.048 --> 00:23:19.732
1960s, Carlos Marcello was facing the severest challenge to his empire yet in the form of the newly elected Kennedy administration.

00:23:20.972 --> 00:23:21.873
President John F.

00:23:21.913 --> 00:23:25.975
Kennedy took office and appointed his brother Robert to the post of attorney general.

00:23:26.816 --> 00:23:28.416
Now with greater power and authority,

00:23:28.837 --> 00:23:32.939
Robert Kennedy and the president continued their campaign to bring down Marcello.

00:23:34.207 --> 00:23:36.628
What Marcello needed was for that threat to go away.

00:23:37.769 --> 00:23:38.569
On November 22,

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:40.250
1963,

00:23:40.251 --> 00:23:41.591
that's exactly what happened.

00:23:43.592 --> 00:23:47.834
President Kennedy was visiting Dallas as part of a Democratic Party campaign tour.

00:23:48.795 --> 00:23:51.716
As a presidential motorcade made its way through Dealey Plaza,

00:23:52.097 --> 00:23:53.597
gunshots suddenly rang out.

00:23:54.618 --> 00:24:03.863
The president was shot and rushed to nearby Parkland Hospital.

00:24:06.583 --> 00:24:06.863
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:24:06.883 --> 00:24:07.323
Within an hour,

00:24:07.764 --> 00:24:08.124
John F.

00:24:08.164 --> 00:24:09.564
Kennedy was pronounced dead.

00:24:13.746 --> 00:24:14.746
A short time later,

00:24:14.827 --> 00:24:18.468
Dallas police apprehended a young man by the name of Lee Harvey Oswald.

00:24:19.909 --> 00:24:22.270
Though quickly fingered as a president's assassin,

00:24:22.710 --> 00:24:24.231
Oswald denied involvement.

00:24:24.951 --> 00:24:29.113
I really don't know what the situation is about.

00:24:29.114 --> 00:24:33.675
Nobody is accused of murdering a policeman.

00:24:34.715 --> 00:24:37.077
Before anyone could thoroughly interrogate Oswald,

00:24:37.117 --> 00:24:37.297
though,

00:24:37.698 --> 00:24:41.842
the Dallas nightclub owner by the name of Jack Ruby slipped through police barricades.

00:24:53.252 --> 00:24:57.095
Amidst the confusion resulting from the killing of President Kennedy and then Oswald,

00:24:57.836 --> 00:25:00.358
numerous conspiracy theories began to emerge.

00:25:01.291 --> 00:25:04.353
The list of possible co-conspirators included the Soviets,

00:25:05.494 --> 00:25:07.956
who had recently backed down in the Cuban Missile Crisis,

00:25:09.036 --> 00:25:09.336
Cuba,

00:25:10.157 --> 00:25:12.138
still angry over the Bay of Pigs invasion,

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:15.821
the CIA,

00:25:16.101 --> 00:25:17.882
whom Kennedy had threatened to disband,

00:25:18.903 --> 00:25:21.425
supporters of slain South Vietnamese President Diem,

00:25:21.885 --> 00:25:24.987
who held the Kennedy administration responsible for Diem's death,

00:25:25.687 --> 00:25:25.807
and,

00:25:25.808 --> 00:25:26.328
of course,

00:25:26.828 --> 00:25:27.389
the Mafia,

00:25:27.889 --> 00:25:30.310
who had more than their share of gripes with the Kennedys.

00:25:31.563 --> 00:25:35.106
The government and the media began taking a closer look at the alleged assassin.

00:25:36.667 --> 00:25:38.309
Oswald often visited New Orleans,

00:25:38.329 --> 00:25:43.273
where he apparently volunteered in a pro-Castro organization called the Fair Play for Cuba Committee.

00:25:44.754 --> 00:25:48.277
Oswald was seen passing out pamphlets for the group on New Orleans streets.

00:25:49.478 --> 00:25:55.463
Oswald's affiliation to this movement was one thing that led many people to speculate on Fidel Castro's involvement in the movement.

00:25:55.463 --> 00:25:56.404
In the assassination,

00:25:57.865 --> 00:26:02.108
another theory linked the assassination to a joint effort between the CIA and Mafia,

00:26:02.168 --> 00:26:03.509
who were both anti-Castro,

00:26:03.689 --> 00:26:05.050
albeit for different reasons.

00:26:05.770 --> 00:26:10.894
The CIA viewed Castro's Cuba as a satellite of the Soviet Union and a threat to national security.

00:26:12.755 --> 00:26:13.555
As for the Mafia,

00:26:13.876 --> 00:26:18.439
Castro was directly responsible for shutting down their lucrative Cuban gambling operations.

00:26:20.411 --> 00:26:22.472
After the disastrous Bay of Pigs invasion,

00:26:22.753 --> 00:26:28.957
both the CIA and the Mafia blamed President Kennedy for the failure of the invasion and for Castro remaining in power.

00:26:30.498 --> 00:26:33.880
Without any concrete evidence to support any of these various theories,

00:26:34.801 --> 00:26:38.543
the controversial Warren Commission concluded that Oswald acted alone.

00:26:39.724 --> 00:26:41.005
Nearly 15 years later,

00:26:41.065 --> 00:26:41.465
however,

00:26:41.466 --> 00:26:42.806
in 1979,

00:26:43.086 --> 00:26:47.009
the House Assassinations Committee decided to reopen the investigation.

00:26:48.390 --> 00:26:49.951
New evidence surfaced to suggest that...

00:26:50.031 --> 00:26:54.575
possibility that Oswald shot the president on behalf of Carlos Marcello.

00:26:56.056 --> 00:26:57.698
By the summer of 1962,

00:26:58.078 --> 00:27:02.101
Robert Kennedy had tightened his grip on the Mafia and brought new charges against Marcello.

00:27:03.142 --> 00:27:06.105
With two federal indictments and a standing deportation order,

00:27:06.365 --> 00:27:09.327
Marcello was living each day under a cloud of uncertainty.

00:27:11.509 --> 00:27:11.629
No,

00:27:11.750 --> 00:27:15.032
I did not expect it.

00:27:15.033 --> 00:27:18.095
We had no indication that anything like this was going to happen.

00:27:18.875 --> 00:27:22.198
new information revealed possible links between Oswald and Marcello.

00:27:23.379 --> 00:27:24.260
While in New Orleans,

00:27:24.580 --> 00:27:25.701
Oswald lived with his uncle,

00:27:25.841 --> 00:27:26.482
Dutz Murrett,

00:27:26.822 --> 00:27:29.664
who was affiliated with members of Marcello's gambling racket.

00:27:31.606 --> 00:27:34.228
Oswald was also possibly connected to David Ferry,

00:27:34.609 --> 00:27:38.872
who was ironically a member of the violently anti-Castro Cuban Revolutionary Front,

00:27:39.573 --> 00:27:42.335
an organization heavily financed by Carlos Marcello.

00:27:43.797 --> 00:27:46.279
Around the time that Kennedy announced his trip to Dallas,

00:27:46.751 --> 00:27:53.597
Carlos Marcello and David Ferry were planning Marcello's defense in a legal case scheduled to go to trial in New Orleans in early November.

00:27:55.779 --> 00:27:58.841
In addition to meeting with Marcello at the Town and Country Motel,

00:27:59.442 --> 00:28:02.505
Ferry also allegedly met with Oswald in the French Quarter.

00:28:04.166 --> 00:28:09.971
Conspiracy theorists speculated that Marcello was looking for someone outside his organization to take care of Kennedy,

00:28:10.812 --> 00:28:13.094
and then learned of Dutz Murrett's oddball nephew,

00:28:13.414 --> 00:28:14.455
Lee Harvey Oswald.

00:28:15.011 --> 00:28:15.291
ROBERT STACKLIN,

00:28:15.292 --> 00:28:16.792
A connection between Oswald's killer,

00:28:16.872 --> 00:28:17.433
Jack Ruby,

00:28:17.553 --> 00:28:19.735
and Carlos Marcello was also discovered.

00:28:20.915 --> 00:28:22.156
In 1946,

00:28:22.377 --> 00:28:25.379
a year before Carlos Marcello assumed power in Louisiana,

00:28:25.879 --> 00:28:32.364
the Chicago mob launched a campaign to take over the rackets in the Dallas area and gain control of the Dallas police force.

00:28:32.945 --> 00:28:34.806
Assisting in the effort was Jack Ruby.

00:28:35.406 --> 00:28:36.667
Ruby stayed on in Dallas,

00:28:36.988 --> 00:28:41.391
opening up a nightclub and acting as a middleman between the mafia and the police department.

00:28:42.547 --> 00:28:46.190
Jack Ruby swore he shot Lee Harvey Oswald to vindicate the president.

00:28:46.690 --> 00:28:47.610
It has been proposed,

00:28:47.630 --> 00:28:48.031
however,

00:28:48.311 --> 00:28:52.494
that Marcello needed someone with access to the Dallas police to silence Oswald,

00:28:52.894 --> 00:28:54.615
and Ruby was a perfect candidate.

00:28:56.857 --> 00:29:04.222
Whether it will be ever definitively determined if Marcello or any other mobster had anything to do with the assassination of John F.

00:29:04.262 --> 00:29:04.602
Kennedy,

00:29:05.202 --> 00:29:10.546
it is fairly safe to say that many of Carlos Marcello's worst headaches went away on November 22,

00:29:10.547 --> 00:29:12.087
1963.

00:29:18.386 --> 00:29:21.148
Long before John Gotti became known as a Teflon Don,

00:29:21.708 --> 00:29:26.210
the head of the New Orleans Mafia was proving to be virtually immune to government enforcement efforts.

00:29:27.171 --> 00:29:27.451
Since the

00:29:28.251 --> 00:29:32.954
1930s, Carlos Marcello had managed to dodge federal probes linking him to organized crime.

00:29:33.774 --> 00:29:36.216
When asked by reporters of his involvement with the rackets,

00:29:36.476 --> 00:29:38.237
Marcello had a standard response.

00:29:38.997 --> 00:29:39.497
it still.

00:29:39.598 --> 00:29:40.918
What was your answer to that?

00:29:41.198 --> 00:29:42.139
Absolutely not.

00:29:43.711 --> 00:29:45.312
When asked about his business ventures,

00:29:45.372 --> 00:29:46.812
Marcello had a stock answer.

00:29:48.193 --> 00:29:50.014
Land investment and motels.

00:29:51.354 --> 00:30:00.738
What's your opinion of all the talk about still your involvement in the Racketts and you're being labeled a mafia boss of the little man of Racketts in Louisiana?

00:30:03.279 --> 00:30:07.921
Just a lot of lies and bad statements from Aaron Cohn,

00:30:08.702 --> 00:30:09.582
people like that.

00:30:10.522 --> 00:30:11.963
He's making a living out of it.

00:30:12.683 --> 00:30:13.924
In 1966,

00:30:13.925 --> 00:30:14.324
however,

00:30:15.104 --> 00:30:17.406
the allegations became impossible to refute.

00:30:18.626 --> 00:30:22.328
Marcello was picked up by New York City police at a restaurant in Queens,

00:30:22.368 --> 00:30:25.070
along with some of the biggest names in organized crime.

00:30:26.371 --> 00:30:28.432
Upon his release and arrival to New Orleans,

00:30:28.512 --> 00:30:31.293
Marcello found himself besieged by reporters and cameras.

00:30:32.094 --> 00:30:37.237
He also placed himself in more legal turmoil after an encounter with FBI agent Patrick Collins.

00:30:37.797 --> 00:30:40.999
He returned to New Orleans in the airport.

00:30:41.847 --> 00:30:42.968
He made one big error,

00:30:43.369 --> 00:30:44.410
a big mistake.

00:30:45.211 --> 00:30:46.032
In the airport,

00:30:46.232 --> 00:30:50.237
he spotted an FBI official,

00:30:52.179 --> 00:30:55.143
and the FBI official came up to him and said,

00:30:55.223 --> 00:30:55.363
Hey...

00:30:55.523 --> 00:30:56.083
Carlos,

00:30:57.484 --> 00:31:01.946
we understand you guys got caught up there in La Stella restaurant.

00:31:02.566 --> 00:31:09.649
We found Marcello hauled off and practically broke the FBI guy's skull.

00:31:10.350 --> 00:31:12.390
That landed him in prison for six months.

00:31:13.311 --> 00:31:15.972
Marcello and his lawyers had their own take on the incident.

00:31:17.033 --> 00:31:19.474
He positively did not know that Mr.

00:31:19.534 --> 00:31:21.374
Pat Collins was an FBI agent.

00:31:21.915 --> 00:31:22.555
And the evidence...

00:31:22.655 --> 00:31:23.936
plainly shows that Mr.

00:31:23.976 --> 00:31:29.779
Pat Collins was a very belligerent young man at that particular moment and he is quoted

00:31:30.599 --> 00:31:36.342
Marcello as saying are you looking for trouble or do you want trouble instead of trying to explain himself

00:31:36.723 --> 00:31:41.685
Mr. Pat Collins says I can handle trouble in a manner to show that he wanted to fight.

00:31:42.766 --> 00:31:52.111
A judge convicted and sentenced Marcello to two years but as he had in the past Marcello used political clout to have his sentence reduced to a mere six months.

00:31:53.511 --> 00:31:55.192
However powerful he was nationally,

00:31:55.533 --> 00:31:59.756
Marcello continually wielded political influence or personal gain on his own turf.

00:32:00.536 --> 00:32:02.758
In an expose about corruption in Louisiana,

00:32:02.978 --> 00:32:07.441
Life magazine looked at Marcello's outrageous exercise of power over state officials.

00:32:08.642 --> 00:32:13.145
The article claimed Marcello persuaded the Louisiana state government to spend taxpayers'

00:32:13.165 --> 00:32:15.067
money to improve his personal

00:32:16.088 --> 00:32:17.509
6,400-acre swampland property,

00:32:17.709 --> 00:32:18.789
Churchill Farms.

00:32:19.510 --> 00:32:20.831
If fully diked and drained.

00:32:20.871 --> 00:32:24.554
the property could be worth a reputed $60 million in prime real estate.

00:32:25.334 --> 00:32:32.219
It took four years and over $1 million to build a levy which benefited no one in the entire state except Carlos Marcello.

00:32:33.840 --> 00:32:37.583
Since even before the days of Louisiana's notorious Senator Huey Long,

00:32:38.124 --> 00:32:41.486
corruption had been an integral part of the state's political system.

00:32:41.886 --> 00:32:48.131
Nobody understands the relationship between politics and corruption in Louisiana better than attorney John Volz.

00:32:50.755 --> 00:32:55.979
His unwillingness to play along may have cost him two campaigns for sheriff and district attorney of St.

00:32:56.039 --> 00:32:56.840
Tammany Parish.

00:32:57.721 --> 00:32:59.822
They give us no break at all.

00:33:00.503 --> 00:33:04.806
I characterize Louisiana as a place that is known for corruption.

00:33:05.767 --> 00:33:06.808
It's known for corruption,

00:33:06.868 --> 00:33:07.328
and why?

00:33:07.929 --> 00:33:10.551
Because there's so many corrupt public officials in this state.

00:33:10.791 --> 00:33:11.372
Not all of them,

00:33:12.212 --> 00:33:12.833
but a lot of them.

00:33:13.153 --> 00:33:13.754
Too many of them.

00:33:14.674 --> 00:33:15.055
And,

00:33:15.895 --> 00:33:16.156
you know,

00:33:16.796 --> 00:33:19.278
it's said that people in Louisiana don't...

00:33:21.067 --> 00:33:22.468
Tolerate corruption and demand it.

00:33:24.228 --> 00:33:34.032
And so they find people who do illegal things but who are glib,

00:33:35.053 --> 00:33:35.793
they find that funny,

00:33:35.873 --> 00:33:36.753
they find it amusing.

00:33:36.993 --> 00:33:37.754
It's entertainment.

00:33:38.674 --> 00:33:39.715
Not all people are like that.

00:33:39.755 --> 00:33:40.335
Not all the people,

00:33:41.035 --> 00:33:43.816
I'm not indicting in the general sense of the word,

00:33:44.957 --> 00:33:48.878
all the citizens of the state because there are some fine people here that don't want to see this.

00:33:49.479 --> 00:33:50.239
But there are too many...

00:33:50.823 --> 00:33:52.645
Too many politicians with their hands out.

00:33:53.305 --> 00:33:57.769
After both the Kefauver Committee in 1951 and the McClellan hearing ten years later,

00:33:58.169 --> 00:34:01.391
Marcello walked away with his empire and reputation intact.

00:34:01.392 --> 00:34:03.553
In 1979,

00:34:03.554 --> 00:34:03.733
though,

00:34:04.054 --> 00:34:11.219
Marcello faced a new challenge when the House Select Committee on Assassinations reopened the investigation into President Kennedy's assassination,

00:34:11.740 --> 00:34:13.981
based on new evidence and new witnesses.

00:34:15.182 --> 00:34:15.703
Ed Becker.

00:34:15.939 --> 00:34:20.941
A Las Vegas casino investigator who moved in and out of mafia circles was one of those witnesses.

00:34:21.821 --> 00:34:25.903
He had occasion to meet with Marcello in 1962 as part of a business venture.

00:34:26.624 --> 00:34:29.565
He was able to gain the mobster's trust by dropping names.

00:34:30.625 --> 00:34:31.646
We were drinking and

00:34:32.686 --> 00:34:33.026
I said,

00:34:33.426 --> 00:34:33.646
boy,

00:34:33.707 --> 00:34:34.207
I'll tell you,

00:34:34.867 --> 00:34:38.629
Bob is sure not happy anymore that you got back in the country.

00:34:39.029 --> 00:34:39.329
I said,

00:34:39.389 --> 00:34:41.170
everybody in Las Vegas is talking about it,

00:34:41.390 --> 00:34:44.071
which of course stimulated his ego.

00:34:45.519 --> 00:34:49.623
And now he's starting to mutter to me about Bobby Kennedy and the Kennedy family.

00:34:50.683 --> 00:34:52.145
He keeps it up and keeps it up,

00:34:52.185 --> 00:34:52.465
and now

00:34:53.046 --> 00:34:53.946
I keep prodding him,

00:34:54.146 --> 00:34:54.447
you know,

00:34:55.868 --> 00:34:56.108
saying,

00:34:57.209 --> 00:34:57.369
well,

00:34:57.409 --> 00:35:00.772
he's not going to be in office that much longer.

00:35:00.792 --> 00:35:01.272
I didn't know.

00:35:02.213 --> 00:35:02.454
I mean,

00:35:02.514 --> 00:35:04.395
Kennedy just had been reelected,

00:35:05.616 --> 00:35:05.896
and

00:35:06.417 --> 00:35:07.198
Bobby was going to,

00:35:07.938 --> 00:35:08.238
I guess,

00:35:08.278 --> 00:35:08.979
go on with him.

00:35:10.280 --> 00:35:11.902
And this infuriated Carlos,

00:35:12.222 --> 00:35:13.503
just even thinking about it.

00:35:13.843 --> 00:35:15.363
That's when he spat out this

00:35:15.884 --> 00:35:16.864
Sicilian phrase,

00:35:16.904 --> 00:35:17.124
which

00:35:18.304 --> 00:35:19.385
I had to do by memory.

00:35:19.425 --> 00:35:19.705
I mean,

00:35:20.025 --> 00:35:21.085
I don't speak Italian,

00:35:21.165 --> 00:35:21.525
so I,

00:35:21.685 --> 00:35:21.945
you know,

00:35:22.005 --> 00:35:22.125
I,

00:35:22.305 --> 00:35:22.706
whatever.

00:35:23.966 --> 00:35:26.167
And phonetically,

00:35:26.227 --> 00:35:28.507
I could grasp it.

00:35:28.547 --> 00:35:30.368
I have a good memory.

00:35:31.528 --> 00:35:35.989
And so I was able to call an Italian consulate one time and find out what it meant.

00:35:36.049 --> 00:35:37.650
And I found out what it meant.

00:35:37.730 --> 00:35:38.090
You know,

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:39.971
you cut off the tail,

00:35:40.171 --> 00:35:40.551
the head,

00:35:40.552 --> 00:35:41.411
and the tail dies.

00:35:44.475 --> 00:35:44.655
Well,

00:35:44.675 --> 00:35:47.557
that means instead of killing the President of the United States,

00:35:47.558 --> 00:35:49.618
we would have been assassinating him,

00:35:49.678 --> 00:35:50.239
which would have been,

00:35:51.079 --> 00:35:51.320
you know,

00:35:51.620 --> 00:35:52.820
the first obvious thought.

00:35:54.361 --> 00:35:54.602
I mean,

00:35:54.622 --> 00:35:55.482
that first obvious thought,

00:35:55.963 --> 00:35:56.463
kill Bobby.

00:35:58.326 --> 00:35:58.946
In the end,

00:35:59.286 --> 00:36:02.567
the House Select Committee on Assassinations came short of indicting him.

00:36:03.487 --> 00:36:03.928
Marcello,

00:36:04.008 --> 00:36:05.488
like so many times before,

00:36:05.548 --> 00:36:07.429
had managed to avoid prosecution.

00:36:07.430 --> 00:36:11.750
In 1979,

00:36:11.751 --> 00:36:12.130
however,

00:36:12.850 --> 00:36:19.072
the wheels of justice would catch up with Marcello as the FBI launched an elaborate undercover sting operation called BRI-LAB,

00:36:19.952 --> 00:36:21.912
an acronym for Bribery and Labor.

00:36:23.253 --> 00:36:27.474
The FBI was able to infiltrate Marcello's inner sanctum through the use of an informer.

00:36:28.654 --> 00:36:34.016
One particular fellow that cooperated was a gentleman by the name of Joe Houser.

00:36:35.557 --> 00:36:36.697
As a result of that,

00:36:37.138 --> 00:36:45.141
we began a series of electronic surveillance activities involving phone taps on

00:36:45.621 --> 00:36:47.102
Marcello's office phones.

00:36:48.422 --> 00:36:49.263
At one point,

00:36:49.723 --> 00:36:57.446
the court authorized electronic surveillance by way of a microphone in his office here in the New Orleans area.

00:36:58.350 --> 00:36:59.111
At one time,

00:36:59.131 --> 00:37:01.352
as I think I remember correctly,

00:37:01.452 --> 00:37:02.933
we even tapped his home phone.

00:37:04.074 --> 00:37:05.575
The FBI recruited Hauser,

00:37:05.755 --> 00:37:11.119
a notorious swindler who at the time was facing a 10-count conviction charge for bribery and racketeering.

00:37:12.200 --> 00:37:12.820
What's in it for me?

00:37:13.360 --> 00:37:15.022
In a deal with the Justice Department,

00:37:15.062 --> 00:37:18.924
Hauser would receive a reduced sentence in exchange for turning government informant.

00:37:19.945 --> 00:37:26.109
The strategy was to present Marcello with an opportunity to assist

00:37:26.590 --> 00:37:26.970
Hauser.

00:37:27.674 --> 00:37:30.356
in obtaining state insurance contracts.

00:37:31.396 --> 00:37:33.397
And he was presented with an opportunity,

00:37:33.497 --> 00:37:34.298
and he took it.

00:37:35.659 --> 00:37:36.839
Not only did he take it,

00:37:36.919 --> 00:37:47.586
but Marcello had his own ideas about how to proceed and who to approach in securing these insurance contracts on state employees.

00:37:49.147 --> 00:37:54.930
What Marcello didn't know was that their conversations were being taped by a recorder hidden in Hauser's briefcase.

00:37:56.390 --> 00:37:57.951
Once those approaches were made,

00:37:57.991 --> 00:37:59.291
we were off to the races.

00:38:00.331 --> 00:38:05.973
And Marcello became a dominant figure in directing Hauser and undercover

00:38:06.353 --> 00:38:16.295
FBI agents who were masquerading as agents who had connections with the Prudential Life Insurance Company.

00:38:23.297 --> 00:38:25.378
In exchange for his influential connections...

00:38:25.658 --> 00:38:31.402
Marcello would receive cash and a cut in profits from any insurance contracts he steered to fidelity prudential.

00:38:33.044 --> 00:38:33.984
The FBI agents,

00:38:34.084 --> 00:38:34.905
the undercover agents,

00:38:34.945 --> 00:38:37.807
were introduced to the then commissioner of administration,

00:38:37.867 --> 00:38:39.749
which is like second to the governor,

00:38:39.989 --> 00:38:40.209
Mr.

00:38:40.249 --> 00:38:40.990
Charles Romer,

00:38:41.350 --> 00:38:46.694
who we alleged and obviously proved that he had taken money,

00:38:46.814 --> 00:38:49.096
I think it was $25,000,

00:38:50.156 --> 00:38:52.078
to steer the state's...

00:38:53.390 --> 00:39:10.159
employees insurance company insurance business to these particular agents and in the process the FBI listened to his conversations for about 19 months in addition to the $25,000

00:39:10.680 --> 00:39:15.663
Marcello promised Romer monthly payments of $43,000 for the life of the contracts

00:39:16.083 --> 00:39:21.366
I think the significance of the case is not only the the

00:39:23.026 --> 00:39:32.094
Prosecution and conviction of a person who is reputed to be way up in the organized crime scale in this area.

00:39:32.735 --> 00:39:42.784
But also the fact that you could see the tentacles of organized crime moving into corrupting state officials.

00:39:43.604 --> 00:39:49.670
Marcello became concerned there was a chance that Romer could lose his position after the Republican gubernatorial primary.

00:39:50.290 --> 00:39:56.356
He decided to hedge his bet on Romer and offer a bribe to then Democratic Lieutenant Governor James Fitzmaurice.

00:39:57.557 --> 00:40:00.840
Marcello believed Fitzmaurice could direct certain contracts his way,

00:40:01.220 --> 00:40:03.482
especially if Romer was removed from his position.

00:40:04.563 --> 00:40:09.507
Fitzmaurice took the bait and accepted Marcello's bribe for $10,000.

00:40:11.569 --> 00:40:16.894
Carlos Marcello's reign as the untouchable godfather of Louisiana ended in August of 1980.

00:40:17.226 --> 00:40:21.247
The grand jury found the 70-year-old Don guilty of numerous racketeering charges.

00:40:21.887 --> 00:40:23.968
Indicted alongside Marcello were Romer,

00:40:24.428 --> 00:40:24.548
I.

00:40:24.568 --> 00:40:25.368
Irving Davidson,

00:40:25.588 --> 00:40:31.470
a longtime friend of Marcello's and an influential Washington lobbyist who had originally introduced Hauser to Marcello,

00:40:32.050 --> 00:40:33.090
and Vincent Marinello,

00:40:33.471 --> 00:40:34.551
a lawyer for Marcello.

00:40:35.671 --> 00:40:38.252
Davidson and Marinello were acquitted of all charges,

00:40:38.412 --> 00:40:41.553
while Romer would be found guilty and sentenced to serve a prison term.

00:40:41.554 --> 00:40:41.813
On June 1,

00:40:41.814 --> 00:40:44.154
1982,

00:40:44.334 --> 00:40:45.374
due to his poor health,

00:40:45.618 --> 00:40:49.951
Marcello was taken to the United States Medical Center for Federal Prisoners in Springfield,

00:40:49.971 --> 00:40:50.392
Missouri,

00:40:50.533 --> 00:40:52.137
to begin serving his sentence.

00:40:53.991 --> 00:40:55.672
After Carlos Marcello's conviction,

00:40:55.893 --> 00:40:59.916
the family's influence in New Orleans political circles and the underworld declined.

00:41:00.897 --> 00:41:01.937
In 1988,

00:41:01.998 --> 00:41:02.778
Carlos's brother,

00:41:02.878 --> 00:41:03.739
Sammy Marcello,

00:41:03.799 --> 00:41:05.240
was indicted for money laundering,

00:41:05.801 --> 00:41:07.582
and several Marcello-owned restaurants,

00:41:07.622 --> 00:41:08.643
like the Louisiana,

00:41:09.003 --> 00:41:10.004
fell into bankruptcy.

00:41:10.865 --> 00:41:14.548
Carlos Marcello died approximately four or five years ago.

00:41:15.308 --> 00:41:17.390
He was succeeded by Anthony Carolla.

00:41:18.151 --> 00:41:20.613
Anthony Carolla was involved in gaming,

00:41:21.153 --> 00:41:22.134
gambling activity,

00:41:22.975 --> 00:41:23.335
and...

00:41:24.283 --> 00:41:26.944
They were meeting at a restaurant in the French Quarter,

00:41:26.964 --> 00:41:27.904
the city of New Orleans,

00:41:28.764 --> 00:41:30.185
and unbeknownst to them,

00:41:30.665 --> 00:41:33.786
they were being watched and monitored by the FBI,

00:41:34.646 --> 00:41:35.066
and the

00:41:35.486 --> 00:41:42.468
FBI actually caught them plotting to take over worldwide gaming and Louisiana route operators.

00:41:43.409 --> 00:41:47.250
One of the people that came from out of town was a gentleman by the name of Tanfield,

00:41:47.690 --> 00:41:50.871
who had connections to the Genovese and Gambino family.

00:41:52.419 --> 00:42:01.447
FBI wiretaps indicate the Gambino and Bruno Scarfo crime families were laying the groundwork for making New Orleans a major port of entry for cocaine smuggling.

00:42:03.248 --> 00:42:03.688
In the early

00:42:04.129 --> 00:42:16.559
90s, the FBI conducted actually a five-year investigation that began in 1990 and ended in the result of the convictions of 20 LCN members and associates.

00:42:17.099 --> 00:42:19.041
The LCN in New Orleans,

00:42:20.522 --> 00:42:21.043
we believe,

00:42:21.083 --> 00:42:21.203
is...

00:42:21.771 --> 00:42:24.533
Pretty much kind of looking at an open city right now.

00:42:24.754 --> 00:42:25.774
What I mean by open city,

00:42:25.854 --> 00:42:34.041
there is not any specifically defined one family that is concentrating on establishing itself in criminal activity.

00:42:40.587 --> 00:42:41.347
In New Orleans,

00:42:41.367 --> 00:42:46.832
a history of affiliation between organized crime and public institutions seems destined to repeat itself.

00:42:47.552 --> 00:42:50.615
I would venture a guess there's corruption here.

00:42:52.547 --> 00:42:53.467
And yes,

00:42:53.547 --> 00:42:54.948
there's corruption in America,

00:42:55.768 --> 00:42:57.489
and I think there always will be.

00:42:59.169 --> 00:43:05.931
But law enforcement should be vigilant and continue to investigate and prosecute corruption.

00:43:07.011 --> 00:43:11.552
It's the only tool available to combat it.

00:43:12.032 --> 00:43:13.933
It will never eradicate it,

00:43:14.173 --> 00:43:14.493
never,

00:43:14.633 --> 00:43:17.614
as long as human beings continue to be human beings.

00:43:18.434 --> 00:43:19.494
But it must be done,

00:43:19.695 --> 00:43:21.115
because I think it tempers.

00:43:22.067 --> 00:43:25.309
restrains excesses.

00:43:25.589 --> 00:43:26.330
On the other hand,

00:43:26.331 --> 00:43:28.551
the government needs to operate within the law,

00:43:30.953 --> 00:43:34.556
and it should always be mindful of that.

00:43:36.417 --> 00:43:37.798
Despite his checkered past,

00:43:38.218 --> 00:43:40.420
the future of New Orleans is looking brighter.

00:43:41.560 --> 00:43:44.963
Over the last four years with the new police chief we do have here,

00:43:45.543 --> 00:43:47.464
we've seen things turn around.

00:43:47.885 --> 00:43:51.167
The quality of life in New Orleans has improved tremendously.

00:43:52.111 --> 00:43:53.572
Crime has dropped.

00:43:53.772 --> 00:43:57.214
The homicide rate has dropped tremendously.

00:43:57.314 --> 00:43:58.074
We were averaging 400,

00:43:58.075 --> 00:44:00.335
430 murders a year.

00:44:01.055 --> 00:44:03.156
It's down probably 40%.

00:44:03.757 --> 00:44:05.297
So across the board,

00:44:06.098 --> 00:44:07.939
the quality of life is improved.

00:44:09.259 --> 00:44:11.780
By exploiting a political system rife with corruption,

00:44:12.481 --> 00:44:15.102
by wielding fear and bribery like precision instruments,

00:44:15.182 --> 00:44:17.083
Marcello was able to amass an empire.

00:44:18.311 --> 00:44:25.394
Only through diligence are honest law enforcement and political officials finally able to bring down the godfather of New Orleans and his dynasty.

00:44:26.635 --> 00:44:27.395
I'm Robert Stack.

00:44:27.995 --> 00:44:28.955
Thanks for joining us.

